      "dom_id": "question-278317",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}=1$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=4$ and $k=-4,$ so the center is located at $(\\,4,-4\\,).$",
